  "content": [
    {
      "type": "block",
      "id": "la-ch02-block-0001",
      "format": "markdown",
      "text": "\n\nSource: https://www.notion.so/40669428c8618122b893d67f62980c36\n\n> Throughout this chapter, $F=\\mathbb R$ or $\\mathbb C$. All vectors in a list belong to the same vector space unless stated otherwise.\n\n"
    },
    {
      "type": "section",
      "id": "la-ch02-section-1",
      "title": "Definitions",
      "label": "1",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "block",
          "id": "la-ch02-block-0002",
          "format": "markdown",
          "text": "\n\n"
        },
        {
          "type": "item",
          "id": "la-ch02-item-1-1",
          "kind": "definition",
          "label": "1.1",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "block",
              "id": "la-ch02-item-1-1-heading",
              "format": "markdown",
              "text": "Span"
            },
            {
              "type": "block",
              "id": "la-ch02-block-0003",
              "format": "markdown",
              "text": "\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{span}(v_1,\\ldots,v_n)=\\left\\{\\sum_{k=1}^na_kv_k:a_1,\\ldots,a_n\\in F\\right\\},\\\\\n\\operatorname{span}(\\varnothing)=\\{0\\}.\n$$\n\n- If $\\operatorname{span}(v_1,\\ldots,v_n)=V$, we say $\\{v_1,\\ldots,v_n\\}$ spans $V$.\n\n"
            }
          ],
          "title": "Span",
          "learning": {
            "capabilities": [
              "recognize",
              "apply"
            ],
            "requires": [],
            "proofCoverage": "none"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "item",
          "id": "la-ch02-item-1-2",
          "kind": "definition",
          "label": "1.2",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "block",
              "id": "la-ch02-item-1-2-heading",
              "format": "markdown",
              "text": "Finite and infinite-dimensionality"
            },
            {
              "type": "block",
              "id": "la-ch02-block-0004",
              "format": "markdown",
              "text": "\n\n- If a vector space $V$ is spanned by a finite number of vectors, $V$ is called finite-dimensional.\n\n- If not finite-dimensional, $V$ is called infinite-dimensional.\n\n"
            }
          ],
          "title": "Finite and infinite-dimensionality",
          "learning": {
            "capabilities": [
              "recognize",
              "apply"
            ],
            "requires": [],
            "proofCoverage": "none"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "item",
          "id": "la-ch02-item-1-3",
          "kind": "definition",
          "label": "1.3",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "block",
              "id": "la-ch02-item-1-3-heading",
              "format": "markdown",
              "text": "Polynomial $\\mathcal{P}(F)$"
            },
            {
              "type": "block",
              "id": "la-ch02-block-0005",
              "format": "markdown",
              "text": "\n\n- It is the set of all the polynomials from $F$ to $F$ with coefficients in $F$.\n\n- The zero polynomial is assigned degree $-\\infty$ and belongs to every $\\mathcal P_m(F)$.\n\n- For a nonnegative integer $m$, $\\mathcal{P}_m(F)$ denotes the set of all polynomials with coefficients in $F$ and degree at most $m$.\n\n"
            }
          ],
          "learning": {
            "capabilities": [
              "recognize",
              "apply"
            ],
            "requires": [],
            "proofCoverage": "none"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "item",
          "id": "la-ch02-item-1-4",
          "kind": "definition",
          "label": "1.4",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "block",
              "id": "la-ch02-item-1-4-heading",
              "format": "markdown",
              "text": "Linear independence and dependence"
            },
            {
              "type": "block",
              "id": "la-ch02-block-0006",
              "format": "markdown",
              "text": "\n\n- A set of vectors $S$ is linearly independent if for every finite list of distinct vectors $v_1,\\ldots,v_k\\in S$ and scalars $a_1,\\ldots,a_k\\in F$, the following is satisfied.\n\n$$\na_1v_1+\\ldots+a_kv_k=0 \\iff a_1=\\ldots=a_k=0\n$$\n\n- $\\varnothing$ is defined to be linearly independent.\n\n- If a set is not linearly independent, it is called linearly dependent.\n\n"
            }
          ],
          "title": "Linear independence and dependence",
          "learning": {
            "capabilities": [
              "recognize",
              "apply"
            ],
            "requires": [],
            "proofCoverage": "none"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "item",
          "id": "la-ch02-item-1-5",
          "kind": "definition",
          "label": "1.5",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "block",
              "id": "la-ch02-item-1-5-heading",
              "format": "markdown",
              "text": "Basis"
            },
            {
              "type": "block",
              "id": "la-ch02-block-0007",
              "format": "markdown",
              "text": "\n\n- A basis of $V$ is a set of vectors in $V$ that is linearly independent and spans $V$.\n\n"
            }
          ],
          "title": "Basis",
          "learning": {
            "capabilities": [
              "recognize",
              "apply"
            ],
            "requires": [],
            "proofCoverage": "none"
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "section",
      "id": "la-ch02-section-2",
      "title": "Theorems",
      "label": "2",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "block",
          "id": "la-ch02-block-0008",
          "format": "markdown",
          "text": "\n\n"
        },
        {
          "type": "item",
          "id": "la-ch02-item-2-1",
          "kind": "theorem",
          "label": "2.1",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "block",
              "id": "la-ch02-item-2-1-heading",
              "format": "markdown",
              "text": "The span of a list is the smallest subspace containing every vector in that list."
            },
            {
              "type": "block",
              "id": "la-ch02-block-0009",
              "format": "markdown",
              "text": "\n\n"
            }
          ],
          "learning": {
            "capabilities": [
              "recognize",
              "apply"
            ],
            "requires": [],
            "proofCoverage": "none"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "item",
          "id": "la-ch02-item-2-2",
          "kind": "theorem",
          "label": "2.2",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "block",
              "id": "la-ch02-item-2-2-heading",
              "format": "markdown",
              "text": "Suppose $v_1,\\ldots,v_m$ is a linearly dependent list in $V$. Then there exists $k\\in\\{1,\\ldots,m\\}$ such that"
            },
            {
              "type": "block",
              "id": "la-ch02-block-0010",
              "format": "markdown",
              "text": "\n\n$$\nv_k \\in \\operatorname{span}(v_1,\\ldots,v_{k-1})\n$$\n\nFurthermore, if $k$ satisfies the condition above and the $k^{th}$ term is removed from $\\{v_1,\\ldots,v_m\\}$, then the span of the remaining list equals $\\operatorname{span}(v_1,\\ldots,v_m)$.\n\n"
            }
          ],
          "learning": {
            "capabilities": [
              "recognize",
              "apply"
            ],
            "requires": [],
            "proofCoverage": "none"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "item",
          "id": "la-ch02-item-2-3",
          "kind": "theorem",
          "label": "2.3",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "block",
              "id": "la-ch02-item-2-3-heading",
              "format": "markdown",
              "text": "In the same vector space, the length of a linearly independent list is at most the length of a finite spanning list."
            },
            {
              "type": "block",
              "id": "la-ch02-block-0011",
              "format": "markdown",
              "text": "\n\n- As a result of this lemma, every subspace of a finite-dimensional vector space is finite-dimensional.\n\n"
            }
          ],
          "learning": {
            "capabilities": [
              "recognize",
              "apply"
            ],
            "requires": [],
            "proofCoverage": "none"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "item",
          "id": "la-ch02-item-2-4",
          "kind": "theorem",
          "label": "2.4",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "block",
              "id": "la-ch02-item-2-4-heading",
              "format": "markdown",
              "text": "A list $v_1,\\ldots,v_n$ of vectors in $V$ is a basis if and only if every $v \\in V$ can be written uniquely in the form"
            },
            {
              "type": "block",
              "id": "la-ch02-block-0012",
              "format": "markdown",
              "text": "\n\n$$\nv=a_1v_1+\\ldots+a_nv_n\\text{ for }a_1,\\ldots,a_n\\in F\n$$\n\n"
            }
          ],
          "learning": {
            "capabilities": [
              "recognize",
              "apply"
            ],
            "requires": [],
            "proofCoverage": "none"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "item",
          "id": "la-ch02-item-2-5",
          "kind": "theorem",
          "label": "2.5",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "block",
              "id": "la-ch02-item-2-5-heading",
              "format": "markdown",
              "text": "Every finite-dimensional vector space has a basis."
            },
            {
              "type": "block",
              "id": "la-ch02-block-0013",
              "format": "markdown",
              "text": "\n\n- Note that every finite spanning list of vectors can be reduced to a basis by theorem 2.2.\n\n"
            }
          ],
          "learning": {
            "capabilities": [
              "recognize",
              "apply"
            ],
            "requires": [],
            "proofCoverage": "none"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "item",
          "id": "la-ch02-item-2-6",
          "kind": "theorem",
          "label": "2.6",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "block",
              "id": "la-ch02-item-2-6-heading",
              "format": "markdown",
              "text": "Every linearly independent set can be extended to a basis of a finite-dimensional vector space."
            },
            {
              "type": "block",
              "id": "la-ch02-block-0014",
              "format": "markdown",
              "text": "\n\n"
            }
          ],
          "learning": {
            "capabilities": [
              "recognize",
              "apply"
            ],
            "requires": [],
            "proofCoverage": "none"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "item",
          "id": "la-ch02-item-2-7",
          "kind": "theorem",
          "label": "2.7",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "block",
              "id": "la-ch02-item-2-7-heading",
              "format": "markdown",
              "text": "For every subspace $U$ of a finite-dimensional $V$, there is a subspace $W$ such that $U \\oplus W=V$."
            },
            {
              "type": "block",
              "id": "la-ch02-block-0015",
              "format": "markdown",
              "text": "\n\n"
            }
          ],
          "learning": {
            "capabilities": [
              "recognize",
              "apply"
            ],
            "requires": [],
            "proofCoverage": "none"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "item",
          "id": "la-ch02-item-2-8",
          "kind": "theorem",
          "label": "2.8",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "block",
              "id": "la-ch02-item-2-8-heading",
              "format": "markdown",
              "text": "The length of any basis of a finite-dimensional vector space $V$ is always the same."
            },
            {
              "type": "block",
              "id": "la-ch02-block-0016",
              "format": "markdown",
              "text": "\n\n- $\\dim(U)\\leq \\dim(V)$ if $U$ is a subspace of $V$.\n\n- This common length is the dimension $\\dim V$, with $\\dim\\{0\\}=0$.\n\n- Every linearly independent list of length $\\dim V$ is a basis of $V$.\n\n- Any spanning subset with the length $\\dim(V)$ is a basis of $V$.\n\n"
            }
          ],
          "learning": {
            "capabilities": [
              "recognize",
              "apply"
            ],
            "requires": [],
            "proofCoverage": "none"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "item",
          "id": "la-ch02-item-2-9",
          "kind": "theorem",
          "label": "2.9",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "block",
              "id": "la-ch02-item-2-9-heading",
              "format": "markdown",
              "text": "If $V_1$ and $V_2$ are subspaces of a finite-dimensional vector space, then"
            },
            {
              "type": "block",
              "id": "la-ch02-block-0017",
              "format": "markdown",
              "text": "\n\n$$\n\\dim(V_1+V_2)=\\dim(V_1)+\\dim(V_2)-\\dim(V_1 \\cap V_2)\n$$\n\n"
            }
          ],
          "learning": {
            "capabilities": [
              "recognize",
              "apply"
            ],
            "requires": [],
            "proofCoverage": "none"
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "section",
      "id": "la-ch02-section-3",
      "title": "Examples",
      "label": "3",
      "content": [
